From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Armada contributors <noreply@localhost>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cpufreq: qcom: expose turbo LUT rows as boost OPPs

Some Qualcomm EPSS LUTs contain one or more single-core turbo rows marked
with LUT_TURBO_IND. The current qcom-cpufreq-hw logic marks those rows
invalid while scanning the LUT, then only restores the final duplicate row
as CPUFREQ_BOOST_FREQ during duplicate/end-of-table handling.

That loses earlier valid turbo rows. If the final turbo row is not present
in the DT OPP table, no boost frequency is exposed even when lower turbo
rows are valid.

Validate each non-duplicate turbo row while scanning the LUT and expose it
as CPUFREQ_BOOST_FREQ when OPP setup succeeds. Keep duplicate terminal rows
invalid so the existing duplicate/end-of-table detection still terminates
the table.

Some firmware exposes a top turbo row in hardware before the corresponding
DT OPP exists. Keep that path off by default, but add an opt-in
qcom_cpufreq_hw.allow_missing_turbo_opp parameter that registers the
LUT-provided freq/volt pair as a dynamic boost OPP when the DT lookup fails
with -ENODEV. This is intended for validated systems only; cpufreq boost
still remains disabled by default.

Link: https://github.com/virtudude/armada/issues/173
---
 drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c | 61 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 60 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff -urN a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c
--- a/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c	2026-06-01 15:54:55.000000000 +0000
+++ b/drivers/cpufreq/qcom-cpufreq-hw.c	2026-07-30 14:39:39.057578057 +0000
@@ -68,6 +68,10 @@
 
 static unsigned long cpu_hw_rate, xo_rate;
 static bool icc_scaling_enabled;
+static bool allow_missing_turbo_opp;
+module_param_named(allow_missing_turbo_opp, allow_missing_turbo_opp, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(allow_missing_turbo_opp,
+		 "Allow LUT turbo rows missing from DT OPP tables to be registered as boost OPPs");
 
 static int qcom_cpufreq_set_bw(struct cpufreq_policy *policy,
 			       unsigned long freq_khz)
@@ -110,6 +114,20 @@
 	return dev_pm_opp_enable(cpu_dev, freq_hz);
 }
 
+static int qcom_cpufreq_add_lut_opp(struct device *cpu_dev,
+				    unsigned long freq_khz,
+				    unsigned long volt)
+{
+	unsigned long freq_hz = freq_khz * 1000;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = dev_pm_opp_add(cpu_dev, freq_hz, volt);
+	if (ret && ret != -EEXIST)
+		return ret;
+
+	return dev_pm_opp_enable(cpu_dev, freq_hz);
+}
+
 static int qcom_cpufreq_hw_target_index(struct cpufreq_policy *policy,
 					unsigned int index)
 {
@@ -207,7 +225,7 @@
 	struct cpufreq_frequency_table	*table;
 	struct dev_pm_opp *opp;
 	unsigned long rate;
-	int ret;
+	int ret, opp_ret;
 	struct qcom_cpufreq_data *drv_data = policy->driver_data;
 	const struct qcom_cpufreq_soc_data *soc_data = qcom_cpufreq.soc_data;
 
@@ -263,7 +281,30 @@
 			}
 
 		} else if (core_count == LUT_TURBO_IND) {
-			table[i].frequency = CPUFREQ_ENTRY_INVALID;
+			if (freq != prev_freq) {
+				opp_ret = qcom_cpufreq_update_opp(cpu_dev, freq, volt);
+				if (opp_ret == -ENODEV && allow_missing_turbo_opp) {
+					dev_info(cpu_dev,
+						 "adding missing turbo OPP from LUT freq=%u volt=%u\n",
+						 freq, volt);
+					opp_ret = qcom_cpufreq_add_lut_opp(cpu_dev, freq, volt);
+				}
+
+				if (!opp_ret) {
+					table[i].frequency = freq;
+					table[i].flags = CPUFREQ_BOOST_FREQ;
+					dev_dbg(cpu_dev,
+						 "index=%d boost freq=%d, core_count %d\n",
+						 i, freq, core_count);
+				} else {
+					dev_warn(cpu_dev,
+						 "failed to update boost OPP for freq=%d ret=%d\n",
+						 freq, opp_ret);
+					table[i].frequency = CPUFREQ_ENTRY_INVALID;
+				}
+			} else {
+				table[i].frequency = CPUFREQ_ENTRY_INVALID;
+			}
 		}
 
 		/*
